  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sahib Singh vs State Of U.P. And Another on 20 January, 2021 Author: Siddharth Bench: Siddharth",
    "facts": "The instant Anticipatory Bail Application has been filed with a prayer to grant an anticipatory bail to the applicant Sahib Singh, in Case Crime No.- 604 of 2020, under Sections- 420, 467, 468, 471 I.P.C., Police Station- Jhinjhana, District- Shamli. Prior notice of this bail application was served in the office of Government Advocate and as per Chapter XVIII, Rule 18 of the Allahabad High Court Rules and as per direction dated 20.11.2020 of this Court in Criminal Misc. Anticipatory Bail Application U/S 438 Cr.P.C. No. 8072 of 2020, Govind Mishra @ Chhotu Versus State of U.P., hence, this anticipatory bail application is being heard. Grant of further time to the learned A.G.A as per Section 438 (3) Cr.P.C. (U.P. Amendment) is not required. Sahib Singh vs State Of U.P. And Another on 20 January, 2021 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/69828084/ 1 The applicant has been implicated for offences under Section 272/273 I.P.C., which are non-bailable offences. Along with the aforesaid sections the applicant has also been implicated for offences under Sections 60 and 63 of Excise Act. It has been submitted that implication under Section 272/273 IPC is unjustified since there is a special Act holding the field, i.e. Food safety and Standards Act 2006. False recovery has been shown against the applicant. The others offences alleged are not made out. The applicant is regularly making efforts to arrest the applicant. The offence under sections 60 of Excise Act is bailable and triable by Magistrate.",
    "arguments": "Learned AGA has opposed the prayer for anticipatory bail of the applicant. He has submitted that in view of the seriousness of the allegations made against the applicant, he is not entitled to grant of anticipatory bail. The apprehension of the applicant is not founded on any material on record. In the case of Joginder Kumar v. State of Uttar Pradesh AIR 1994 SC 1349 the Apex Court has referred to the third report of National Police Commission wherein it is mentioned that arrests by the police in India is one of the chief source of corruption in the police. The report suggested that, by and large, nearly 60 percent of the arrests were either unnecessary or unjustified and that such unjustified police action accounted for 43.2 percent of expenditure of the jails. Personal liberty is a very precious fundamental rights and it should be curtailed only when it becomes imperative. According to the peculiar facts and circumstances of the peculiar case the arrest of an accused should be made. Without expressing any opinion on the merits of the case and considering the nature of accusation and his antecedents, the applicant is entitled to be released on anticipatory bail for limited period in this case considering the exceptions considered by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in the case of Sushila Aggarwal vs. State (NCT of Delhi)- 2020 SCC Online SC 98.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "Learned AGA has opposed the prayer for anticipatory bail of the applicant. He has submitted that in view of the seriousness of the allegations made against the applicant, he is not entitled to grant of anticipatory bail. The apprehension of the applicant is not founded on any material on record.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In the case of Joginder Kumar v. State of Uttar Pradesh AIR 1994 SC 1349 the Apex Court has referred to the third report of National Police Commission wherein it is mentioned that arrests by the police in India is one of the chief source of corruption in the police. The report suggested that, by and large, nearly 60 percent of the arrests were either unnecessary or unjustified and that such unjustified police action accounted for 43.2 percent of expenditure of the jails. Personal liberty is a very precious fundamental rights and it should be curtailed only when it becomes imperative. According to the peculiar facts and circumstances of the peculiar case the arrest of an accused should be made. Without expressing any opinion on the merits of the case and considering the nature of accusation and his antecedents, the applicant is entitled to be released on anticipatory bail for limited period in this case considering the exceptions considered by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in the case of Sushila Aggarwal vs. State (NCT of Delhi)- 2020 SCC Online SC 98."
  },
